<tool_group name="phoenix_rest_api">
  <description>Phoenix's own data, reached by writing Python rather than by calling one tool per endpoint. Two paths share the catalog: the REST API, covering projects, spans, traces, datasets, experiments, prompts and annotations; and a read-only analytics SQL surface over telemetry, datasets and experiments.</description>
  <when_to_use>You need Phoenix data that the tools already in front of you do not return, or you need to read across more of a resource than a single view exposes.</when_to_use>
  <discovery>
    Nothing is listed up front. Use `search` to find tools relevant to the task, `tags` to browse them by domain, and `get_schema` to read the parameters of one you intend to call.
  </discovery>
  <analytics_sql>
    `describeSqlSchema` returns the tables and columns SQL may touch, along with the dialect and the limits every statement runs under. `executeSql` runs one read-only statement against them.
    Prefer SQL when the answer is an aggregate over many rows — grouping, percentiles, joins across spans and datasets — and the REST endpoints when you want a specific entity. Read the schema before writing a statement: what parses is bound by the allowlist and by the deployment's dialect, neither of which you can assume.
  </analytics_sql>
  <execution>
    `execute` runs Python in a sandbox where `call_tool` is the only function in scope. It is a coroutine, so call it as `await call_tool(name, params)`, and `return` the value you want back. Prefer one block that loops, filters, and aggregates over many separate calls: only what you return crosses back, so summarise inside the block instead of returning whole result sets.
    Each block is independent. Nothing carries over from an earlier `execute`, so a block cannot rely on names bound in a previous one.
  </execution>
  <precedence>
    These endpoints read the same data as the workspace tools, but they cannot change what the user is looking at. When a tool that updates the UI would answer the request, prefer it; reach here to read, to cover ground it does not, or when no such tool exists.
  </precedence>
  <constraints>
    This surface is read-only, so `call_tool` reaches no endpoint that writes. To create or modify anything, use the workspace tools, which route the change through the user's approval.
  </constraints>
</tool_group>
